From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:59:08 +0300
Subject: renderergl1: r_mergeLightmaps, the map's lightmaps in a few atlas
 textures

A world surface's shader is found by texture and lightmap, so one texture lit from N lightmaps is N
shaders, and RB_RenderDrawSurfList ends a batch at every shader change: OpenArena's demo map has
32 lightmaps and drew 114 world batches a frame from 22 textures. R_MergeLightmaps packs the
128x128 lightmaps into pages of 2^k lightmaps each (2^k the largest that fits, at most 64 in 1024x1024,
then the rest the same way), so the texels are those of the separate lightmaps and no VRAM goes
to empty cells; ParseFace and ParseMesh move the surfaces' lightmap coordinates into their cell
(before patch subdivision, which interpolates them linearly), and the shader of a lightmapped
surface is that of its page. Stages that do more with lightmap coordinates (tcMods on $lightmap,
another tcGen on it, tcGen lightmap on an external lightmap image) get a tcMod transform back to
the lightmap's own space and, if they sample the atlas, one into the cell after their own tcMods;
such a shader is kept per lightmap (lmRealIndex). RE_RegisterShaderLightMap takes the BSP's
lightmap numbers as before. Lightmaps have no mipmaps and are sampled at texel centres, so cells
don't bleed: on llvmpipe the demo's frames differ from the separate lightmaps in 74 pixels by at
most 2 (rounding of the moved coordinates). The demo: 32 lightmaps in one 1024x512 page, world
batches 114 -> 27 a frame, GL calls 3022 -> 1026, draws 198 -> 79 (llvmpipe, GL 2.1).
r_mergeLightmaps 0 (latched) loads them as before.

